+// [SAMPLING] THE SAMPLE TRANSFER CONTROLLER, I/O 0xC000-0xC03F.
+//
+// [FALSIFIED 2026-08-27] THIS IS NOT THE RECORD PATH. With logging live and Sample -> Go pressed,
+// the firmware made ZERO accesses to this block. The block is real - `BA 01 C0` occurs 35 times in
+// the ROM against 0.031 expected by chance - but it is not what recording waits on, so the reading
+// below is kept only as a record of how it was traced. The mapping stays because storage for a real
+// register block is correct either way, and it now logs if anything ever does touch it.
+//
+// The original (wrong) reasoning follows.
+// This block was entirely unmapped, and it was thought to be why "Sample -> Go" wedges the machine: the display
+// reaches "RECORDING analog ..." and then sits at 0 % forever with F8/ABORT dead. It is not a crash,
+// it is a wait that cannot end. The driver's own TODO list already said "Recording."
+//
+// HOW IT WAS FOUND, because a naive search does not find it. Scanning the 512 KB OS ROM for byte
+// patterns produced nothing usable: a control showed 525 "hits" in an I/O window with NOTHING mapped
+// against 74 in the real one, and 256 expected purely by chance - so the whole scan was noise and was
+// thrown away. What worked was a linear disassembly correlating every `in`/`out` with the nearest
+// preceding `mov dx,imm`, validated against ports we already model (0x50/0x52/0x54 KLCS, 0x20/0x22
+// FDC, 0x82/0x84 L7A1045 - all present, so the disassembly is in sync through the code regions).
+// That found 261 accesses to this block, none of which had anywhere to go.
+//
+// THE WAIT ITSELF, at 0xEF47D:
+//     ef47f: mov dx,0C001h / out dx,al     select counter 0
+//     ef483: mov dx,0C004h / in  ax,dx     read the position
+//     ef487: mov si,[8570h] / cmp ax,si
+//     ef48d: ja  0EF496h                   proceed once the position passes the target
+//     ef494: jmp 0EF47Dh                   otherwise go round again
+// and the target is built at 0xEF3FE as `and bp,3FFh` - the low ten bits of the 32-bit sample
+// address. The length register at 0xC002 is written as min(len, 0x400) - 1 (0xE00B6). So the engine
+// fills a 1024-word page and this counter reports how far into that page it has got. Unmapped, it
+// reads 0, the comparison never passes, and the firmware waits forever.
+//
+// THE RATE IS NOT A GUESS. The L7A1045 is fitted with a 33.8688 MHz crystal and divides it by 768,
+// which is 44100 Hz exactly - the same figure the firmware prints on its own record screen. A page is
+// 1024 words, so the counter wraps every 23.2 ms.
+//
+// REPORTED ONLY FOR COUNTER 0, the one the record loop selects. Every other selection keeps the
+// behaviour it has today, so no currently working path changes - and that is a decision with a
+// falsifier attached: if recording now runs but sample PLAYBACK breaks, then 0xC004 is not
+// per-counter the way this assumes. There is already a reason to expect other selections report
+// something else: 0xD431B reads it and then does `sub ax,91B0h`, which no 10-bit value would justify.
+//
+// Everything else in the block keeps honest STORAGE rather than reading back zero. Not a nicety: the
+// firmware does read-modify-writes here (0xC00F is set and cleared bit by bit at 0xEEBF0 and 0xEEBF8),
+// and a register that reads back zero deletes whatever was just configured.
+//
+// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not carry audio. The counter moves, so the firmware can finish its
+// wait and the panel comes back, but the recorded data is silence until the MICROPHONE device and the
+// transfer address registers are modelled. That is the next step and it is named rather than faked.
+// [SAMPLING 27] THE INPUT CONTROL PORT, $0180 - found by watching the machine rather than reading a ROM.
+//
+// The look-back ring caught the last writes before the very first A/D word moved:
+//     IOW-BACK $0180 = 00A2  pc=15CFD  t=93.20780
+//     IOW-BACK $0180 = 0082  pc=15D09  t=93.20780
+//     IOW-BACK $0180 = 008A  pc=14301  t=93.24294     <- and the transfer begins at 93.2429
+//
+// Decoded against the bit layout this driver has documented since before any of this work, for the
+// same control port on the S3000/CD3000i at I/O 0x0068:
+//     bit 0  analog/digital input select, or digital input enable
+//     bit 1  LEFT channel input DMA enable
+//     bit 2  RIGHT channel input DMA enable
+//     bit 3  input control strobe or reset
+// so 0x82 is LEFT enabled, 0x8A is LEFT enabled plus the strobe, and BIT 2 IS CLEAR IN ALL OF THEM.
+// That was a MONO recording, and the machine asked for the left channel only - while this model was
+// handing it left, right, left, right regardless. Every second sample came from a channel the machine
+// had not enabled, which is precisely a recording that is recognisable but distorted, and it is why
+// mono and stereo behaved differently throughout.
+//
+// The upper bits (0x80, and 0x20 in 0xA2) are not decoded here. They are not guessed at either: they
+// are stored and left alone, because a register that reads back what was written cannot corrupt the
+// firmware's own read-modify-writes, and inventing meanings for bits is how earlier rounds went wrong.
+uint8_t s3000_state::inctl_r()
+{
+	return m_inctl;
+}
