+//      run happens at t=4.12, while the machine sits parked from t=12 to t=40 (PCHIST: 271B0, 270C6,
+//      271A3, 271AD, 271A6 all climbing linearly). So it is NOT polling a missing register. It is
+//      waiting for something that has to be PUSHED to it, and on this machine that means DMA.
+//   2. THE SCHEMATIC. IC50 is an AK5339-VP, "16 bit dual A/D convertor with 64 fs oversampling
+//      digital filter". Its output is SERIAL (SDATA, with ADLR selecting left/right), it goes into
+//      the IC30 PAL, and what comes out of that PAL is DMRQ2 / DAK2. The sample data never touches a
+//      CPU port, which is exactly why no `in`/`out` search was ever going to find it.
+//   3. THE DRIVER. in_ior_cb<0> is the SCSI, <1> is the floppy, in_io16r_cb<3> is the L7A1045.
+//      CHANNEL 2 IS WIRED TO NOTHING.
+//
+// The wiring here follows s1000.cpp, which is R. Belmont's own solution for the same problem on the
+// same manufacturer's sampler: MAME's generic adc16_device, fed from a MICROPHONE through a mixer,
+// read by the DMA controller, with the sample clock raising DREQ and DACK clearing it.
+//
+// TWO SIMPLIFICATIONS, both stated rather than hidden:
+//   - DREQ2 is raised by a free-running 44100 Hz clock (33.8688 MHz / 768, the L7A1045's own crystal
+//     divided the way that device divides it - the same 44100 the firmware prints on its record
+//     screen). On the real board the PAL gates DMRQ2 with WADCS and OPDETN. An ungated request is
+//     harmless while the DMA channel is masked, which it is unless the firmware has set recording up,
+//     so the visible behaviour should match. FALSIFIER: if anything else starts using channel 2, or
+//     transfers appear when no recording was asked for, the gate is needed and this is why.
+//   - ADLR is modelled as strict left/right alternation on successive DMA reads. That is the natural
+//     reading of a single serial line carrying a stereo pair, but it is NOT confirmed from the
+//     schematic. FALSIFIER: a recording that comes out with the channels swapped, or one channel
+//     silent, points straight back here.
+uint16_t s3000_state::adc_dma_r()
+{
+	// [DIAGNOSTIC, SAMPLING 17 - REVERT AFTER THE ANSWER] adc16::read() forces a full MAME stream
+	// update, walking mixer -> microphone -> resampler -> the plugin's ring, and this path does it
+	// 88200 times a second. Measured: the emulation runs at ratio 1.001 until recording starts and
+	// then sits at 2.560, which with HOST=44100 known means 39 percent of real time. That is a 68000
+	// frame per second surplus, and no drift correction can touch it - mine was sized for the few
+	// frames per second that drift actually costs, which was a hundredfold error of magnitude on my
+	// part.
+	//
+	// This build answers ONE question and nothing else: is the stream update the cost? With the read
+	// removed the recording is silence by construction, so judge SPEED only - the ratio in the log.
+	// If it returns to 1.0 the A/D path is the load and the fix is to stop updating per word; if it
+	// stays at 2.5 the cost is elsewhere and I have been blaming the wrong thing.
+	// [SAMPLING 18] DIAGNOSTIC REVERTED, QUESTION ANSWERED. With the read removed the emulation ran at
+	// ratio 1.000, 0.998, 1.000; with it in place it sits at 2.560. So adc16::read() forcing a full
+	// MAME stream update 88200 times a second IS the load, confirmed rather than assumed.
+	//
+	// The read is back because silence is worse than glitching, and the correct fix is a different
+	// shape: a small sound consumer that receives the microphone in BLOCKS at MAME's own cadence and
+	// buffers into a FIFO the DMA drains, instead of forcing a full chain walk per word. That is also
+	// what the hardware does - the converter runs continuously into the PAL and the DMA takes what is
+	// there - so it is closer to the part, not merely faster.
+	// A frame is popped once, on its LEFT word; the right word is handed out from the same frame. That
+	// is what one ADLR cycle is, and it also means one FIFO pop per frame instead of two stream walks.
+	// A frame is popped once, on its LEFT word; the right word comes from the same frame, so the pair
+	// can never be assembled from two different instants. The phase comes from the clock, not from a
+	// toggle here, so a word the DMA never collects costs one word and nothing after it.
+	// [SAMPLING 27] WHICH CHANNELS THE MACHINE ASKED FOR, from $0180 bits 1 and 2. With both enabled a
+	// frame is popped on its left word and the right word comes from the same frame, so the pair can
+	// never be assembled from two instants. With only one enabled - which is what a MONO recording
+	// does, measured - every transfer is that one channel and a frame is popped for each, because the
+	// machine is taking one sample per frame, not two.
+	// [SAMPLING 38] THE CHANNEL GATE IS REVERTED - the machine's own display falsified it.
+	//
+	// SAMPLING 27 read $00C0 bits 1 and 2 as LEFT and RIGHT input DMA enable, taken from a comment this
+	// driver carries for the S3000/CD3000i port at 0x0068. Measured on the XL: with the screen showing
+	// RECORD STEREO and 293792 samples - exactly 2 x 146896 frames - INCTL still reports LEFT-DMA=1 and
+	// RIGHT-DMA=0. Bit 2 is never set, in stereo or in mono. So on this machine those bits do not mean
+	// what that comment says, and building the transfer on them made a stereo recording receive left
+	// words only, one new frame per transfer, which the machine then read as an L,R,L,R pair sequence.
+	// Left, left, left taken as a stereo pair is noise, and that is the garbage Thor heard.
+	//
+	// The converter simply alternates: it clocks a left word then a right word, every frame, and the
+	// DMA takes as many as the firmware programmed it to take. That is what the AK5339's L/R output pin
+	// does and it needs no permission from a control latch. m_inctl is still read and logged, because
+	// knowing what the machine writes there is worth having - it is just no longer used to gate.
+	// [SAMPLING 39] ONE DMA WORD IS ONE FRAME. The alternation above is reverted, and this is the
+	// first version of this handshake that was settled by measuring the RESULT rather than by reading
+	// a register and reasoning forwards from it.
+	//
+	// Measured, on Thor's capture of a sine tone through the sampler (sine3.wav, the SAMPLING 38
+	// build): 34294 runs of length exactly 2 in a 44100 Hz stream, 16932 of them aligned to an even
+	// index against 163 to an odd one, and the recovered pitch 63.79 Hz where the tone went in near
+	// 127.6. Every sample delivered twice, in fixed phase, and the pitch exactly halved. Raw:
+	//
+	//   577, 577, 532, 531, 486, 485, 440, 440, 393, 393, 347, 347, 301, 301, ...
+	//
+	// That is the signature of a consumer taking ONE word per frame while the producer hands out two.
+	// Thor's reading of the firmware says the same thing from the other end: the record page selects
+	// LEFT or RIGHT, one side, and the PAL turns that choice into either LDMA or RDMA driving DMRQ2.
+	// One request line, one word per frame. The pair never travels down this path together.
+	//
+	// So: pop a frame per transfer and hand over the selected side. The consumption rate now equals
+	// the converter's frame rate by construction, which makes this self-checking - if the machine
+	// really did want two words per frame the FIFO would starve at exactly 2:1 and ADC-HEALTH would
+	// say so in the first second, rather than the defect hiding as a pitch error the way it just did.
+	// [SAMPLING 111] TWO WORDS PER FRAME IN STEREO, ONE IN MONO - and the mode is now MEASURED.
+	//
+	// SAMPLING 39 settled on one word per frame and closed with the claim that this was self-checking,
+	// because a machine wanting two would starve the FIFO at 2:1 and say so. It cannot say so: pop()
+	// HOLDS the last frame when starved instead of reporting a shortfall, and the request is only ever
+	// raised while fill() > 0, so 44100 words a second is the ceiling THIS CODE imposes. The same
+	// reading would have come back if the machine wanted 88200. That is the S3000XL SAMPLING 39 rule
+	// turned on its own author, in the very function the rule was written in.
+	//
+	// The evidence that is NOT capped by this side is the machine's own screen, recorded in SAMPLING 47:
+	// STEREO, tim 1.00s = 88200 - the firmware stating that one second of stereo is 88200 words, into
+	// the single contiguous buffer at $0091B0 that the ADC-WRAP probe measured. Two words per frame,
+	// collected in one place, which is exactly what a serial converter with an L/R output pin produces.
+	//
+	// So the ceiling comes off rather than a new assumption going in. In stereo a frame is popped on its
+	// LEFT word and the RIGHT word is handed out from the same frame, so a pair can never be assembled
+	// from two different instants; in mono every transfer pops a frame and takes the left word, exactly
+	// as before. SAMPLING 38 also alternated L and R and halved the pitch - the difference is that it
+	// alternated WITHOUT doubling the offer rate, so two transfers consumed two frames instead of one.
+	//
+	// FALSIFIER, and it is a real one: if the machine only ever wanted one word per frame, the second
+	// word is simply never collected, the FIFO backs up and ADC-GAP and fill() will show it. That is an
+	// observation this construction cannot force, which is the whole point of taking the ceiling off
+	// rather than lowering it. Mono is untouched, because bit 4 is clear there.
+	// [SAMPLING 122] THE RIGHT WORD IS HANDED OVER AGAIN, and this time the input path either side of
+	// it is PROVEN rather than assumed. With a stereo signal in, measured at two points in the chain:
+	//
+	//   SAMPLER-IN  PEAK-L=100.0%  PEAK-R=100.0%          the right channel reaches the plugin
+	//   ADC-PEAK    LEFT 32766 (100.0%)  RIGHT 32766      and reaches the converter, at full scale
+	//
+	// So the microphone wiring and the FL/FR mapping are fine - Thor's mono-input hypothesis is
+	// falsified - and this line is the ONLY place in the whole chain where the right channel is lost.
+	// It is also why the monitor has no right side during a recording: the machine never receives a
+	// right word at all. Until SAMPLING 121 neither meter could have shown this, because both took
+	// max(|L|,|R|) and a silent side read exactly like a live one.
+	//
+	// Gated on $00C0 bit 4, so MONO IS BYTE-FOR-BYTE THE PATH THAT RECORDS WELL TODAY.
+	uint16_t data;
+	if (m_adc_stereo && m_adc_half)
+	{
+		data = uint16_t(m_adc_r & 0xffff);   // the right half of the frame already popped
+		m_adc_half = 0;
+		m_adc_right_words++;
+	}
+	else
+	{
+		if (m_adcfe.found())
+			m_adcfe->pop(m_adc_l, m_adc_r);
+		data = uint16_t((m_adc_right_source ? m_adc_r : m_adc_l) & 0xffff);
+		m_adc_half = m_adc_stereo ? 1 : 0;   // [SAMPLING 122] the right word completes this frame
+		m_adc_left_words++;
+	}
+
+	m_adc_tick++;
+	m_adc_lr = m_adc_half;
+
+	// [SAMPLING 46] Mark the window in which the controller writes this word to memory. The sequence
+	// per transfer is: read the I/O side (here), write memory, assert DACK. A single bool is all the
+	// write tap may cost - the Virus taught this project that machine().time() in a per-access handler
+	// is what turns an emulation into a slideshow.
+	m_adc_xfer = true;
+
+	// [SAMPLING 25] THE REQUEST FOLLOWS THE DATA, NOT THE CLOCK.
+	//
+	// SAMPLING 24 offered a word only if the FIFO held one AT THE INSTANT OF A TICK. Frames arrive in
+	// blocks though - about eleven every 250 microseconds - so between blocks the FIFO is empty, those
+	// ticks produced no request, and the word they would have carried was lost for good. Measured: the
+	// DMA took 70402 words a second against the 88200 produced, so the machine received four words in
+	// every five and the recording came out distorted. Gating on a clock edge can only ever lose the
+	// difference; gating on the data cannot.
+	//
+	// So: drop the line for this word, then raise it again immediately if another word is already
+	// waiting. Back-to-back words drain back-to-back, a block is taken as a block, and the transfer
+	// stops exactly when the converter has nothing left - which is what DMRQ2 does on the board. The
+	// 88200 Hz tick stays as the restart after a genuine gap; it no longer sets the pace.
+	m_maincpu->dreq_w<2>(0);
+	m_adc_req_pending = false;
+
+	// [SAMPLING 58] restored - see the revert note in tout1_w().
+	// [SAMPLING 111] a right word already in hand needs no frame, so it is always available.
+	const bool next_available = (m_adc_half != 0) || (m_adcfe.found() && m_adcfe->fill() > 0);
+	if (next_available)
+	{
+		m_adc_req_pending = true;
+		m_maincpu->dreq_w<2>(1);
+	}
+
+	// Open the I/O write window the moment the first word moves: whatever set this up was written
+	// just before, and whatever changes it will be written just after.
+	if (m_adc_words == 0)
+	{
+		m_iowin_until = machine().time().as_double() + 2.0;
+
+		// Dump the last 64 I/O writes, oldest first. Whatever armed this transfer is in here.
+		if (!m_iow_dumped)
+		{
+			m_iow_dumped = true;
+			S3000_LOG("IOW-LOOKBACK the last 64 I/O writes before the first A/D transfer, oldest first:\n");
+			for (int i = 0; i < 64; i++)
+			{
+				const auto &e = m_iow_ring[(m_iow_ri + i) & 63];
+				if (e.t > 0.0)
+					logerror("  IOW-BACK $%04X = %04X pc=%05X t=%.5f\n", e.port, e.data, e.pc, e.t);
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
+	// [SAMPLING 39] THE WORD RATE IS THE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDES THIS. One word per frame means
+	// 44100 words a second; if the machine is really taking a stereo pair it will take 88200 and the
+	// FIFO will run dry. Report the rate over the last wall second and the FIFO depth beside it, so
+	// the next run answers the question outright instead of leaving it to a pitch estimate afterwards.
+	++m_adc_words;
+	// [SAMPLING 152] GOLD: the A/D meters read the clock on EVERY word. ADC-GAP, ADC-RATE and the
+	// burst counters answered their questions - the 88200/s split, the gaps, the starvation that was
+	// never there - and none of them is needed to record. Set S3000_AD_METERS to 1 to investigate.
+	const double now = S3000_AD_METERS ? machine().time().as_double() : 0.0;
+	if (m_adc_words <= 4)
+		S3000_LOG("ADC-DMA #%llu word=%04X t=%.4f\n", (unsigned long long)m_adc_words, data, now);
+
+	// [SAMPLING 40] WHERE THE CLICKS ARE, measured rather than reasoned about. Thor's capture of a
+	// 100 Hz tone through the sampler (sine4.wav) has the duplication gone and the pitch right, but
+	// carries periodic clicks: 32 of them in the left channel and 30 in the right over 7.4 s, with a
+	// median spacing of 4469 samples on BOTH channels - 101.3 ms, 9.87 Hz. A defect that lands at the
+	// same interval on both channels is not noise in the signal, it is a SEAM in the transfer.
+	//
+	// A seam has a signature this side can see directly: a gap in the DMA word stream. The nominal
+	// spacing is one word every 22.68 us, so anything past three times that is the transfer stopping
+	// and restarting - the DMA hitting its programmed count and being re-armed, most likely, since
+	// out_eop_cb on this machine reaches only the floppy's TC merger and nothing of ours.
+	//
+	// So: report the gap, its length and the word it follows. If the gaps come at 4469 words the seam
+	// IS the buffer boundary and the click is what we do across it; if they do not, the click is in
+	// the data and this instrument says so by staying quiet. Both answers are useful, which is the
+	// property U533 on the Virus established a probe needs - trigger on the signature, do not sample.
+	// [SAMPLING 41] THE 40-LINE BUDGET WENT TO THE BULK WRITER, AGAIN. SAMPLING 40 triggered on any
+	// break longer than three word periods and filled all forty lines inside five milliseconds:
+	//
+	//   ADC-GAP #2 after word 1377 (11 since the last gap): 0.193 ms  fifo=10  t=45.60126
+	//   ... every entry identical, 11 words then 0.193 ms, one every 250 us ...
+	//
+	// 11 words per 250 us is 44000 words a second - that is MY OWN 4 kHz FIFO refill tick, not a seam
+	// in the machine's transfer. The probe measured the producer's cadence and spent the whole budget
+	// on it. This is the Virus U508 failure verbatim: a budget shared between a bulk writer and a rare
+	// event is always spent on the bulk writer. The threshold now sits well above the tick.
+	if (m_adc_words > 1)
+	{
+		const double gap = now - m_adc_last_word_t;
+
+		// A REAL seam. The 4 kHz tick costs 0.193 ms; anything past 2 ms is the transfer itself
+		// stopping, which is what a buffer boundary or a re-arm looks like from this side.
+		if (gap > 0.002 && m_adc_gaps < 40)
+		{
+			++m_adc_gaps;
+			S3000_LOG("ADC-GAP #%u after word %llu (%llu since the last gap): %.3f ms  fifo=%u  t=%.5f\n",
+				m_adc_gaps, (unsigned long long)m_adc_words - 1,
+				(unsigned long long)(m_adc_words - 1 - m_adc_gap_w0), gap * 1000.0,
+				m_adcfe.found() ? m_adcfe->fill() : 0u, now);
+			m_adc_gap_w0 = m_adc_words - 1;
+		}
+
+		// [SAMPLING 41] THE ONE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLES WORDS-PER-FRAME, and it needs Thor's screen to
+		// read it, which is the point - this side cannot see how long the machine thinks the sample is.
+		//
+		// SAMPLING 39 claimed the rate was self-checking and it was not: DREQ is only raised when the
+		// FIFO holds a frame and the FIFO fills at 44100 a second, so 44100 words a second is the
+		// CEILING THIS CODE IMPOSES, not a fact about the machine. starved=0 is empty for the same
+		// reason - the machine cannot ask when there is nothing to give. An instrument whose reading is
+		// fixed by its own construction reports nothing, and I presented it as the answer.
+		//
+		// What cannot be forced by this side is the ratio between the words transferred and the sample
+		// length the machine ends up with. A quiet period of 250 ms ends a recording; report the words
+		// and the elapsed time. Then: words / seconds / 44100 is 1 if the machine takes one word per
+		// frame and 2 if it takes a stereo pair, and the sample length on the panel says which of those
+		// is consistent with what the firmware believes it recorded.
+		//
+		// The manual settles the intent, and it overturns SAMPLING 39's reading: in STEREO the machine
+		// records BOTH channels and appends -L and -R to the two samples. The LEFT/RIGHT field beside
+		// the mode is NOT a source select - it chooses which channel TRIGGERS the recording when INPUT
+		// LEVEL is used to start it. So a stereo recording wants two words per frame, and Thor's own
+		// observation is the other half of the evidence: the elapsed time on screen ran out of step with
+		// real time while the audio still came out 3 seconds long in memory. That is exactly what a
+		// transfer starved to half its rate looks like from the front panel.
+		if (gap > 0.05 && m_adc_burst_w0 != 0)
+		{
+			const uint64_t words = m_adc_words - 1 - m_adc_burst_w0;
+			const double secs = m_adc_last_word_t - m_adc_burst_t0;
+			if (words > 4410)
+				S3000_LOG("ADC-BURST %llu words in %.4f s = %.1f words/s = %.3f words per frame at 44100  t=%.5f\n",
+					(unsigned long long)words, secs, secs > 0 ? words / secs : 0.0,
+					secs > 0 ? words / secs / 44100.0 : 0.0, now);
+			m_adc_burst_w0 = 0;
+		}
+		if (m_adc_burst_w0 == 0)
+		{
+			m_adc_burst_w0 = m_adc_words - 1;
+			m_adc_burst_t0 = now;
+		}
+	}
+	m_adc_last_word_t = now;
+
+	if (m_adc_words > 4 && now - m_adc_rate_t0 >= 1.0)
+	{
+		// [SAMPLING 111] the mode and the L/R split go on the line, so the reading can be checked
+		// against what the machine asked for instead of against what this code was built to give.
+		S3000_LOG("ADC-RATE %llu words/s  %s  L=%llu R=%llu  (44100 = one word per frame, 88200 = a stereo pair)  fifo=%u  t=%.4f\n",
+			(unsigned long long)(m_adc_words - m_adc_rate_w0),
+			m_adc_stereo ? "STEREO" : "MONO  ",
+			(unsigned long long)m_adc_left_words, (unsigned long long)m_adc_right_words,
+			m_adcfe.found() ? m_adcfe->fill() : 0u, now);
+		m_adc_rate_t0 = now;
+		m_adc_rate_w0 = m_adc_words;
+	}
+	return data;
+}
+
+void s3000_state::adc_dack_w(int state)
+{
+	// [SAMPLING 51] WHICH LEVEL MEANS ACKNOWLEDGED? am9517a::set_dack() hands the SELECTED channel
+	// COMMAND_DACK_ACTIVE_HIGH and the other three its complement, and that command bit belongs to the
+	// firmware. This handler has assumed state==1 means "our transfer completed" since the channel was
+	// first wired. If the machine programs DACK active LOW then state==1 is the opposite - it fires
+	// whenever ANOTHER channel is being serviced - and this handler drops our DREQ every time the SCSI,
+	// the floppy or the L7A1045 moves a byte. The A/D transfer would then be interrupted by other
+	// traffic on a regular cadence, which is the shape of the 4468-sample grid we have been chasing.
+	//
+	// This does not act on that. It counts both levels against the A/D's own word count, and the
+	// arithmetic decides: state==1 arriving roughly once per A/D word means active high and the
+	// handler is correct; state==1 arriving far more often than there are A/D words means active low
+	// and it has been backwards all along.
+	// [SAMPLING 52] MEASURED, AND IT IS ACTIVE LOW. Per emulated second, against 44100 A/D words:
+	//
+	//   state=0  +88189   which is 2 x 44100, exactly twice per A/D word - our channel's own transfer
+	//   state=1  +1058411 which is every OTHER channel being serviced
+	//
+	// So state==0 is the acknowledgement and this handler has had it backwards since the channel was
+	// first wired. On state==1 it was executing dreq_w<2>(0), dropping the A/D's request more than a
+	// million times a second, every one of them while the SCSI, the floppy or the L7A1045 held the
+	// bus. The transfer survived only because adc_dma_r() raises the request again after each word,
+	// so what was left was a request being cancelled and reinstated out of step with the machine's
+	// own bus traffic - which is the shape of a periodic loss, and a periodic loss on a grid near but
+	// not on the 4096-word buffer wrap is exactly what has been measured in every recording.
+	//
+	// Kept because it is grounded, not guessed: the counts are two-to-one against the word rate and
+	// twenty-four-to-one the other way, and no reading of an active-high DACK produces those numbers.
+	if (!state)
+	{
+		m_maincpu->dreq_w<2>(0);
+		m_adc_req_pending = false;
+		// [SAMPLING 45] stamp it, so eop_w() can tell the A/D's terminal count from the other three
+		// channels' - out_eop_cb is shared by all four and says nothing about which one fired.
+		m_adc_dack_t = machine().time();
+		m_adc_xfer = false;
+		m_last_dack_adc = true;
+	}
+}
+
+TIMER_CALLBACK_MEMBER(s3000_state::adc_clock_tick)
+{
+	// [SAMPLING] THE FRAME BOUNDARY RE-ANCHORS ADLR, and this is the fix for the symptom Thor named
+	// exactly: recorded in stereo, the signal wanders between the two sides "as if an LFO were on the
+	// pan". That is not a pan, it is PHASE DRIFT between two nearly equal rates.
+	//
+	// The first version flipped m_adc_lr on every DMA read and nothing else. Free-running alternation
+	// has no reference: one missed transfer, one aborted recording, one odd word count, and left and
+	// right swap PERMANENTLY - and when that happens repeatedly the swap sweeps, which is what a slow
+	// pan sounds like. On the real board ADLR is a SIGNAL FROM THE CONVERTER saying which half of the
+	// frame the current word belongs to; it cannot drift because the frame defines it.
+	//
+	// So the frame defines it here too: every sample clock starts a new frame, and a frame starts on
+	// the left. Now a lost word costs one frame instead of every frame after it.
+	// A REQUEST MUST NOT OUTLIVE ITS FRAME. The first version asserted DREQ2 and left it asserted
+	// until a DACK arrived. If no DACK ever comes - the firmware masks the channel, aborts the
+	// recording, or simply never programmed it - the line stays high for ever and the DMA controller
+	// starves the CPU. The machine then wedges on GO with two words transferred, which is exactly what
+	// the log showed: ADC-DMA #1 and #2 and nothing after.
+	//
+	// This was written down as a risk when the free-running clock went in ("an ungated request is
+	// harmless while the DMA channel is masked... FALSIFIER: if transfers appear when no recording was
+	// asked for, the gate is needed") and the falsifier has now fired. It is not harmless.
+	//
+	// On the real board DMRQ2 comes out of the IC30 PAL as a PULSE per converted frame, not a level
+	// held until someone answers. So an unanswered request is dropped at the next frame boundary and
+	// costs one frame, which is what the hardware does.
+	if (m_adc_req_pending)
+	{
+		m_maincpu->dreq_w<2>(0);
+		// [SAMPLING 31] The channel has gone away after a real transfer: this is the moment the
+		// firmware starts waiting for completion, so open the read window here.
+		if (!m_ioread_armed && m_adc_words > 1000)
+		{
+			m_ioread_armed = true;
+			m_ioread_until = machine().time().as_double() + 0.5;
+			S3000_LOG("IOR-WINDOW opened at t=%.5f after %llu words - the transfer has stopped\n",
+				machine().time().as_double(), (unsigned long long)m_adc_words);
+		}
+
+		if (++m_adc_unacked <= 4 || (m_adc_unacked % 44100) == 0)
+			S3000_LOG("ADC-DREQ unacknowledged #%llu - request dropped at the frame boundary t=%.4f\n",
+				(unsigned long long)m_adc_unacked, machine().time().as_double());
+	}
+
+	// [SAMPLING 21] ONE REQUEST PER WORD, NOT PER FRAME. A stereo frame is TWO words, and when
+	// SAMPLING 15 made the request a pulse that is dropped at the next boundary it also limited the
+	// transfer to ONE word per boundary. Measured: ADC-DMA #1 at t=49.2426 and #44100 at t=50.2422 -
+	// exactly 44100 WORDS in one second, which is 22050 stereo frames against the 44100 the converter
+	// produces. The machine was being handed half its samples, and half a stereo stream taken as a
+	// whole one is stutter.
+	//
+	// On the board the PAL raises DMRQ2 once per word: ADLR toggles within the frame and each half
+	// gets its own request. So this timer runs at 88200 Hz, and the LEFT/RIGHT phase is taken from the
+	// tick counter rather than from a toggle - which keeps the anti-drift property of SAMPLING 10,
+	// where a lost word costs one word and cannot invert the pairing for everything after it.
+	// [SAMPLING 22] THE PHASE FOLLOWS DELIVERED WORDS, NOT TICKS. SAMPLING 21 advanced ADLR on every
+	// clock tick, and that is wrong for one specific reason: we raise a request on EVERY tick, including
+	// the vast majority when the DMA channel is not running at all, and those go unacknowledged. The
+	// phase marched on regardless, so when a recording finally started the DMA picked it up at an
+	// arbitrary half, and every dropped request during the recording flipped it again. Left and right
+	// interleaved at random is not stutter, it is noise - which is exactly what Thor heard, "like an
+	// 8-bit C64 noise effect".
+	//
+	// So the tick only ever OFFERS the current word. The phase advances in adc_dma_r(), once per word
+	// actually handed over, so an unanswered request costs nothing and the pairing cannot invert. A
+	// recording therefore always begins on a left word.
+	// [SAMPLING 24] NO SAMPLE, NO REQUEST - the rate limit belongs at the converter, not in a timer.
+	//
+	// Measured: the DMA drained 44100 words in 0.265 s, which is 166415 words a second against the
+	// 88200 the converter produces, and the FIFO reported starved with fill 0 tens of thousands of
+	// times. The machine was pulling roughly twice as fast as samples existed, getting held repeats for
+	// the difference - a recording stretched out and stitched from duplicates, which is exactly
+	// "slowed down with a glitch matrix effect".
+	//
+	// The cause is that the V53's DMA does not take one word per request: in demand or block mode a
+	// single DREQ moves as many words as it can while the line is high, and DACK does not pulse per
+	// word. Raising one request per word therefore did not limit anything.
+	//
+	// The PAL does not have this problem because it only raises DMRQ2 WHEN THERE IS A WORD TO TAKE. So:
+	// a left word is offered only when the FIFO actually holds a frame; a right word is always
+	// offered, because it completes a pair already popped. That caps the transfer at the converter's
+	// own production rate by construction, with no timer arithmetic to get wrong.
+	// [SAMPLING 39] One word is one frame now, so there is no free half-pair and no phase to keep.
+	// [SAMPLING 111] in stereo there is again a half-pair, and it is kept in m_adc_half rather than
+	// being recomputed here - the phase advances only on words actually handed over, so an unanswered
+	// request still costs nothing and the pairing still cannot invert.
+
+	// [SAMPLING 58] restored - see the revert note in tout1_w().
+	const bool have_word = (m_adc_half != 0) || (m_adcfe.found() && m_adcfe->fill() > 0);
+	if (have_word)
+	{
+		m_adc_req_pending = true;
+		m_maincpu->dreq_w<2>(1);
+	}
+}
+
+// [SAMPLING] WHICH PORTS DOES IT ACTUALLY POLL, and WHERE DOES IT SIT.
+//
+// Two rounds of reading the ROM produced a confident answer that the machine then refused: the record
+// wait was placed at 0xEF47D on port 0xC004, the model was built, and with logging live the firmware
+// made ZERO accesses to that block. So the reading was wrong, and no amount of further reading fixes
+// that - the machine has to be asked.
+//
+// unmapped_io_r/w catch everything the io map does not claim, deduped on (pc, port, direction) so a
+// poll that spins a million times is ONE line. Reads return ZERO, which is what MAME's own unmapped
+// read returns (addrmap.cpp m_unmapval(0)), so behaviour is unchanged.
+//
+// pcsample_tick is the other half: a port list says WHAT is being read, a pc histogram says WHERE the
+// processor is standing. The histogram is keyed on FREQUENCY, not on consecutive samples - that
+// distinction cost a whole round on the Virus, where a trigger built on 20 consecutive identical pcs
+// produced nothing at all while 85 % of samples sat on one address.
+uint16_t s3000_state::unmapped_io_r(offs_t offset, uint16_t mem_mask)
+{
+	static std::set<uint32_t> seen;
+	const uint16_t port = uint16_t(offset * 2);
+	const uint32_t key = (uint32_t(m_maincpu->pc()) << 8) ^ (uint32_t(port) << 4);
+	if (seen.size() < 200 && seen.insert(key).second)
+		S3000_LOG("IO-UNMAPPED-R $%04X  mask=%04X pc=%05X t=%.4f\n",
+			port, mem_mask, m_maincpu->pc(), machine().time().as_double());
+	// ZERO, matching MAME's own unmapped value. An instrument that alters the machine is not an
+	// instrument - and this one did, for exactly one build.
+	return 0x0000;
+}
+
+// [SAMPLING 44] THE TERMINAL COUNT, MADE VISIBLE - and it forwards the line untouched, so this is an
+// observation and not a change.
+//
+// SAMPLING 43 measured two grids in the recorded ramp: one striking exactly every 4096 samples, a
+// power of two and therefore a buffer length, the other every 4471, with the 375 difference showing up
+// as the drift between them. At each strike the stored content jumps back about a buffer length -
+// content re-recorded, not input lost.
+//
+// The question this answers is narrow and it comes before any fix: does the A/D transfer reach
+// terminal count at all, and if it does, how many words apart. If EOP arrives every 4096 words then
+// the 4096 grid IS the DMA wrap and the 4471 one is a firmware service on its own cadence, and the
+// missing signal between them is what we never built - out_eop_cb has only ever reached the floppy's
+// TC merger. If EOP never arrives during a recording then the wrap is not a terminal count at all and
+// the 4096 has some other source, which would rule the DMA out rather than in.
+//
+// Both answers are worth having, and neither is available by reading the ROM: the OS runs from RAM,
+// so the code that services this buffer cannot be disassembled from the image. Same rule as the DSP's
+// P-RAM on the Virus - it has to be measured from running code.
+void s3000_state::eop_w(int state)
+{
+	// Forward first, unchanged and inverted, exactly as the original wiring did.
+	m_tc->in_w<0>(!state);
+
+	// [SAMPLING 45] ATTRIBUTE THE EOP TO A CHANNEL, because out_eop_cb does not.
+	//
+	// SAMPLING 44's reading was useless for the reason the v5x makes plain in its own header: there is
+	// ONE eop callback for all four channels. Measured: 64 EOPs per 341 A/D words, so roughly five
+	// sixths of them belong to the SCSI, the floppy or the L7A1045 and the A/D's own terminal count was
+	// buried in them. A counter that cannot tell whose event it counted is not a measurement - the same
+	// error as SAMPLING 40's gap probe counting my own refill tick.
+	//
+	// The DMA asserts EOP on the SAME transfer as the final DACK, so the channel-2 DACK's timestamp
+	// identifies it exactly. Only those are reported, and each one says how many A/D words fitted in
+	// the transfer - which is the buffer length, the number this whole hunt is about.
+	// [SAMPLING 50] ATTRIBUTE THE EOP EXACTLY, because both previous attempts shaped their own answer.
+	//
+	// SAMPLING 45 required machine().time() to EQUAL the channel-2 DACK timestamp. am9517a.cpp asserts
+	// EOP in STATE_S3 and calls set_dack() in STATE_S2, one state earlier, so the two can never be
+	// equal; the predicate matched nothing and SAMPLING 46 read that silence as the transfer never
+	// reaching terminal count. Retracted at SAMPLING 49.
+	//
+	// SAMPLING 49 then reported only EOPs with more than a thousand A/D words behind them, and the log
+	// came back:
+	//
+	//   ADC-EOP #3  1025 A/D words, 23.220 ms      #4  1023 words, 23.220 ms      #5  1022 words ...
+	//
+	// Every value between 1022 and 1026, sitting just above my own threshold of 1000, and 23.220 ms is
+	// exactly 1024/44100. That is the filter's floor, not the machine's period: reporting the FIRST
+	// event after each thousand-word window manufactures a thousand-word rhythm whatever the machine
+	// does. Third instrument in this session whose reading was fixed by its own construction.
+	//
+	// The structure gives an exact answer with no threshold in it. EOP is asserted during the final
+	// transfer of a channel, and that transfer's DACK was raised one state earlier - no other channel
+	// can be interleaved inside a single transfer. So the channel that owns the EOP is simply the
+	// channel whose DACK went up last. All four are tracked now: channel 2 sets the flag, the SCSI, the
+	// floppy and the L7A1045 clear it, and the floppy's TC merger still receives its own line as it
+	// always did. No timestamp comparison, no budget, no window.
+	if (state && m_last_dack_adc && m_adc_words > 0)
+	{
+		const double now = machine().time().as_double();
+		if (++m_eops <= 40)
+			S3000_LOG("ADC-EOP #%llu  %llu A/D words in this transfer, %.3f ms  t=%.5f\n",
+				(unsigned long long)m_eops, (unsigned long long)(m_adc_words - m_eop_w0),
+				(now - m_eop_t0) * 1000.0, now);
+		m_eop_t0 = now;
+		m_eop_w0 = m_adc_words;
+	}
+}
+
+// [SAMPLING 50] The other three channels' DACKs, so the EOP can be attributed. Channel 1 keeps its
+// original job of feeding the floppy's TC merger; 0 and 3 were not wired to anything before.
+// [SAMPLING 56] TOUT1 GATES THE PAL THAT MAKES THE DMA REQUEST - from the schematic Thor photographed.
+//
+// IC30, the PALCE20V8Q marked L6039B on the main board, carries these and nothing else from the DMA:
+//
+//   /DAK2 -> I8        an INPUT, the channel-2 acknowledge
+//   DMRQ2 <- Y21       the OUTPUT, the channel-2 request
+//   /FBSY -> I/CK      the clock
+//   TOUT1 -> I/G       the OUTPUT ENABLE
+//   LDMA, RDMA, 22/44, 384FS, /WADCS, /IORD, BTCK, SDLRD, OPDETN, A1, D0, D1
+//
+// Two things follow. First, THERE IS NO TERMINAL COUNT ON THIS PAL - no TC, no EOP pin - so the
+// firmware does not learn from it that the ring has turned, and the search for that path has to look
+// elsewhere. Second, and this is the modelling gap: the V53's TIMER 1 OUTPUT enables the PAL's
+// outputs, DMRQ2 among them. On the board the A/D's DMA request only exists while that timer permits
+// it. In this driver TOUT1 goes to IRQ6 and nothing else, so our request line is free-running and
+// synchronised to nothing - which is the shape of a copy-out and an overwrite drifting across each
+// other, and SAMPLING 55 measured what that costs: 58 per cent of the recording is material stored
+// more than once.
+//
+// NOTHING IS GATED YET. The rate and duty of TOUT1 are not known, and gating a request line on a
+// signal whose behaviour is a guess is how this session produced four instruments that reported their
+// own construction. This observer forwards the line to IRQ6 exactly as before and reports what the
+// timer actually does, once a second, against the A/D's own word count.
+void s3000_state::tout1_w(int state)
+{
+	m_maincpu->set_input_line(INPUT_LINE_IRQ6, state ? ASSERT_LINE : CLEAR_LINE);
+
+	// [SAMPLING 58] THE TOUT1 PACING IS REVERTED. It made the machine freeze at RECORD - Thor's word
+	// was "unusable" - so whatever else is true of it, it is not what the board does with this model
+	// attached to it. REVERT-GUESS does not care that a change was grounded in a schematic; a change
+	// that breaks the machine comes out, and it comes out immediately rather than being defended.
+	//
+	// What the observation leaves behind, and it is worth keeping: TOUT1 toggles 44199 times a second
+	// from boot, constantly, independent of recording. Against IC30's I/G pin that makes it a per-sample
+	// enable rather than a per-buffer gate. And 44199 against the converter's 44100 is a 0.22 per cent
+	// difference between two clocks that on the board are BOTH derived from the same 44.1 kHz lock -
+	// IC21, the AV9173 genlock PLL, takes WCK441 and LR441 and produces MCK and 384FS, and 384FS is what
+	// reaches the PAL. In this model they are two independent clocks. That is a real discrepancy and it
+	// is the next thing to examine, but examining it is not the same as pacing a DMA request on it.
+	if (state) m_tout1_hi++; else m_tout1_lo++;
+	const double now = machine().time().as_double();
+	if (now - m_tout1_report_t >= 1.0)
+	{
+		m_tout1_report_t = now;
+		S3000_LOG("TOUT1 rose %llu times, fell %llu times, level now %d, against %llu A/D words  t=%.4f\n",
+			(unsigned long long)m_tout1_hi, (unsigned long long)m_tout1_lo, state,
+			(unsigned long long)m_adc_words, now);
+	}
+}
+
+void s3000_state::other_dack_w(int state)
+{
+	// [SAMPLING 52] active low, measured - see adc_dack_w()
+	if (!state)
+		m_last_dack_adc = false;
+}
+
+void s3000_state::fdc_dack_w(int state)
+{
+	if (!state)
+		m_last_dack_adc = false;
+	m_tc->in_w<1>(state);      // unchanged - the floppy's TC merger gets exactly the line it always did
+}
